=== ch3_receiving
~ tick_n = 4
->> tick
@comp room
@set receiving
@cam main
@acoustic tiles
@amb receiving_room
@xwin 1 off
if inez_no_look:
    @view plan
    She walks you through by telling you. You get the plan of it without her eyes: a small square room, tiled floor to ceiling, a basin, a drain, a handset on the wall with a line you can feel, cold.
else:
    @view inez
    @comp call
    @cam inez_eye
Behind the deep end there's a door in the tiled wall, painted the same cream as the wall so you'd miss it. An enamel plate on it that somebody has scraped the paint off: RECEIVING.
Inside, white tiles, small ones, floor and walls and ceiling. A shallow porcelain basin shaped like a reclining chair, sitting on a plinth, with a drain under it. A brass meter on the wall with a coin slot and a crank. A cream handset on a bracket with a number on the cradle: 201. A towel rail with no towels. A mirror gone brown at the edges.
It's colder than the pool. You can tell because she puts her hands in her armpits.
INEZ: Receiving room. Nineteen fifty-one to seventy-nine. About a hundred and forty people came through this.
@spot cradle 0.30 0.50 0.34 0.34
@spot meter 0.70 0.30 0.12 0.18
@spot photos 0.06 0.18 0.22 0.24
@spot poster 0.44 0.10 0.18 0.30
@spot register 0.82 0.56 0.14 0.16
@spot notes 0.66 0.52 0.14 0.14
-> ch3_receiving_look

=== ch3_receiving_look
* [look][spot:photos] The photographs.
    ~ k_photos = true
    if inez_no_look:
        @text_doc inez photo_arrivals "1958. They're in the pool steps."
        @text_doc inez photo_attendants "1965. Look at the caps."
    @show_doc photo_arrivals
    @show_doc photo_attendants
    @gallery_add photo_arrivals
    @gallery_add photo_attendants
    INEZ: Arrivals, fifty-eight. That lot were all one winter. The one laughing is Mrs. Doyle. She lived on the third floor till ninety-four.
    INEZ: And the attendants. Sixty-five.
    Eleven men in a row along the pool edge, in the same jacket and the same cap, photographed so they come out the same.
    * [look] Look closer.
        They aren't the same. One has a ring on his little finger. One's cap is on crooked. The fourth from the left is smiling, and the one next to him is holding his hands together in front of him like someone still waiting to be told something.
        ~ k_attendants_differ = true
    * "Who were they?"
        INEZ: The register'll tell you. Such as it tells you anything.
    -> ch3_receiving_look
* [read][spot:register] The ledger.
    ~ k_register = true
    It's in a plastic bag on a shelf, a green cloth ledger, the kind with a red spine. Columns: Number. Date. Name as registered. Called for by. Remarks.
    @show_doc register_page
    @gallery_add register_page
    Most of the remarks say Registered. Some say Registered, housed. A run of them, 1964 to 1966, say something else.
    INEZ: People rang the Baths asking for the attendant. Complaints, lost property, somebody's kid won't get out of the water. Ask for the attendant.
    INEZ: So an attendant turned up. Eleven times. And the council gave them caps.
    * "Did they want caps?"
        INEZ: It doesn't say. It says appointed. It doesn't say asked.
        ~ k_appointed_precedent = true
    * "Registered and appointed."
        INEZ: In the same line. Same ink. Somebody saved themselves a column.
        ~ k_appointed_precedent = true
    -> ch3_receiving_look
* [read][spot:poster] The poster.
    @show_doc poster_received
    @gallery_add poster_received
    ~ k_poster = true
    A Ministry of Health poster in a clip frame. A diagram of a body, stiff and careful, drawn by somebody who'd seen a body described. THE RECEIVED PERSON: WHAT TO EXPECT IN YOUR FIRST WEEK.
    INEZ: I like the bit about the mirror.
    -> ch3_receiving_look
* [look][spot:notes] Notes stuck to the tiles.
    Index cards, taped straight onto the tiles, going brown in layers. Cradle valve repacked 3/77. A.R. Meter reset 14.2.71. And one in blue marker, newer than the rest: 2009 FLOOD. DO NOT RUN BOILER ABOVE 3. I.C.
    INEZ: A.R. was the attendant before me. Before the council forgot about us. He wrote everything down. I write everything down because he did.
    ~ k_notes = true
    -> ch3_receiving_look
* [look][spot:meter] The brass meter.
    -> ch3_token
* [look][spot:cradle] The basin.
    INEZ: The cradle. Warm feed from the old boiler, through the meter. You lie in it, or you... arrive in it, I suppose. There isn't a proper word. The ledger says received.
    ~ k_cradle = true
    -> ch3_receiving_look
* {k_register and k_photos and k_token_talk} "Show me what it does."
    -> ch3_demo

=== ch3_token
~ k_token_talk = true
A slot, a crank, a little window with a dial in it stuck at zero. Stamped under the slot: F.S.B. RECEIVING. INSERT TOKEN.
if k_token_seen:
    ARI: There's a token in the bag. In G/1. It says receiving.
    INEZ: I know. I put it there.
else:
    INEZ: There's a token that goes in that. It's in your bag, in G/1. I put it there.
* "Why?"
    INEZ: For luck. I had a feeling, and no reason. I don't like feelings without reasons.
    INEZ: I put it in the bag so I'd stop having it.
* "You knew?"
    INEZ: I knew 247 was doing something. I didn't know it was someone.
    INEZ: That's not an excuse. It's just what I knew.
* "It's not a locker token."
    INEZ: The lockers took pennies.
~ k_token_receiving = true
~ k_inez_knew = true
-> ch3_receiving_look

=== ch3_demo
INEZ: I've got the engineer's key. It's not the token. It gives you a test: ten seconds, no more. The token's for the real thing.
There's a wall box by the door with its own row of jacks. She takes a patch cord out of her pocket and holds it up where you can see it, or where you can hear it, a little rattle of brass.
INEZ: Two-four-seven to two-oh-one. Ready?
* "Ready."
* "What will it feel like?"
    INEZ: I've no idea. I've never been on that end.
    INEZ: Ready?
    * "Ready."
@sfx patch_in
@sfx water_tremble
@state cradle on
@state fog on
SOUND: A jack going home. A pipe knocking, then water moving, not much, in the basin.
Weight. That's first. Something has weight, and it's you.
An elbow, on something curved and cold. Then a knee. Then the idea of a back. Warm water round the elbow, and the air cold on everything else. For a second there's a smell, and you've never had a smell: chlorine, rust, the wool of her coat.
The tiles over the basin fog in a patch the length of a person.
@sfx patch_out
@state cradle off
SOUND: The jack comes out.
And all of it goes, the way a hand comes off your shoulder.
THINK: For three seconds you had an elbow.
~ k_demo = true
* "Again."
    ~ demo_again = true
    INEZ: No.
* "Don't do that again."
    ~ demo_no = true
    INEZ: I won't. Not without you asking.
* Say nothing.
    INEZ: Ari?
    ARI: I'm here.
    INEZ: Good. Right. Good.
INEZ: That's what the room's for.
INEZ: For the real thing you need three: you on 201, the token in the meter, and somebody on that handset for twenty minutes, keeping you talking. They called it holding the place.
INEZ: If they put the phone down, it stops, and you're back on the line. Nothing's lost. It just stops.
~ k_hold_place = true
~ k_arrivals = true
* "What happens after?"
    INEZ: You're a person with a body and no paperwork. You're hungry, and it's cold, and you need a name on a form and a bed. That's the part the council used to do.
    INEZ: Now it's Sal with a folder.
* "Who holds the place?"
    INEZ: Whoever you ask. Whoever says yes.
    INEZ: Not me by myself at two in the morning with no witness and no idea what you want.
* "Do I have to?"
    INEZ: You don't have to anything. Nobody ever made anybody lie in that. Not even the council.
    INEZ: Although they did give them caps.
@state fog off
@spots_clear
-> ch3_teodor

